WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.000
Good morning, everybody.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.060
And welcome to the next installment of the
Big Data to Knowledge Guide to the Fundamentals

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.840
of Data Science.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.610
This is Mark Musen from Stanford University.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:16.740
And I have the pleasure of introducing today's
speaker who is a Professor Isaac Kohane from

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.200
the Harvard Medical School.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.570
Dr. Kohane has a distinguished career.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:26.710
He's currently the founding Chair of the Department
of Biomedical Informatics at Harvard and has

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:32.980
a long history of working in areas that range
from the molecular to the population level.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:39.390
He is probably best known, most recently,
for his work as the guy who led the development

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:45.980
of i2b2, which is a major resource which is
now used in over 120 academic health centers

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.650
around the world as a way of managing both
clinical and molecular data.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:55.220
We know that in the world of big data we talk
about volume, velocity, and variety.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.340
And clinical data have all of that.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.560
And it's a pleasure to introduce Zak to your
today's talk.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.610
Thank you very much.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.570
It's my pleasure to be here.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.220
My name is Isaac Kohane.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:11.140
I am called by most people Zak.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:19.610
And I'm glad to talk to you today about considerations
of critical data for precision medicine.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.720
Before I go further, I just want to check
with my organizers that you can see my slide--

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.810
that looks like the precision medicine slide--
and that you can hear me.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:30.950
It looks good.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.950
Good.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:32.950
Thank you.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:41.600
So this is a slide just to remind you of what
precision medicine was about, at least, what

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.280
we think it's about for those of us-- as I
was privileged to be-- who were members of

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:54.520
the National Academy of Sciences committee
that helped write the precision medicine report.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:03.590
In a nutshell, we declared that we had Google
Maps MV that-- as shown on the left of the

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:10.869
screen-- we saw that by taking latitude and
longitude and combining it with a variety

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:24.650
of other data types-- namely, for example,
weather, pharmacies, traffic-- we would create

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:31.849
an ecosystem-- they created an ecosystem--
that now has everybody engaged in applied

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:34.480
geography internationally.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:42.700
And we said that if we were to create a similar
information commons that was centered around

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:49.460
patients that gave this multiaxial view of
patients-- not only in one study at a time,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:57.650
whether from, let's say, environmental exposure,
or clinical times and symptoms, or microbial

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:05.860
contents of your gut, but brought all together--
we would hope to create a similar productive

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:14.360
ecosystem by looking at each patient from
a much more well-rounded perspective rather

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:20.970
than the blocky kind of categorical view that
we have currently in medicine.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.959
Some of the points I will make in my comments
today will be that most population genetics

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:25.849
and clinical medicine is
Some of the points I will make in my comments

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:32.830
today will be that most population genetics
and clinical medicine is addicted to categorical

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.080
diagnosis.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:44.810
And that addiction creates some false or wrongly
taxonomized diseases and makes social and

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:50.860
commercial construction of disease likely--
sometimes for good, sometimes for ill.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:56.489
I also want to make the point that clinical
predictive accuracy does not imply sheer physiology,

00:03:56.489 --> 00:04:02.690
and that disease overlaps demonstrate a fundamental
problem, which precision medicine and its

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:07.680
approaches are trying to solve, and that clinical
data can be used to lessen the confusion.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:16.329
I want to briefly show here some ROC curves
from a paper I published several years ago

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:25.990
about using the transcriptum of white blood
cells in patients with autism to distinguish

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:31.270
them from individuals who did not have autism.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:39.371
And the only reason I point this out is that
these fairly good looking AUC curves-- for

00:04:39.371 --> 00:04:45.320
a brain disease, at least, as measured by
blood transcription-- caused me to be invited

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:50.760
to another school in my neighborhood, the
Harvard Law School.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:59.630
And when I entered that meeting, I saw an
individual who I was reminded that I thought

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:06.130
I had seen him on TV, on network TV, in the
past week or two.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.590
And I couldn't figure out who it was.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.280
I just couldn't remember.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.130
And then it came back to me when he asked
me the following question-- why are you trying

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:19.060
to commit genocide on my people?

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:26.650
This guy is Ari Ne'eman, who has a form of
autism.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:34.360
And he picks the view that a lot of these
efforts for diagnosing autism amount to trying

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:39.460
to filter out these individuals and eliminate
them from our population.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.199
And therefore this constitutes a form of genocide.

00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:44.850
And I had a lot of discussion.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:49.479
But it really made me think about the diagnosis.

00:05:49.479 --> 00:05:55.570
But I would rather-- instead of voicing my
own comments-- voice comments of a woman that

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:58.610
was public about these on the web.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:03.600
And she said, "I think when Ari talks about
autism and I talk about autism, we're talking

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.800
about people with different clusters of autism.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.570
I know he doesn't like that word 'cure.'

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:12.449
If my daughter would function the way Ari
could, I would consider her cured," said Singer.

00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:17.830
"I have to believe my daughter doesn't want
to be spending time peeling skin off her arm."

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:23.919
So the point here I am making is this diagnostic
label can actually mean a lot of different

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:26.420
things to many different people.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:35.350
And therefore, when we use them, for example,
to do a study of autism versus not autism,

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:40.630
the nature of that label is going to determine
how successful we are.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:45.840
Just as doing a study of heart failure versus
not heart failure would be very different

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:52.970
if we focused it merely on heart failure due
to atherosclerosis or all the heart failures--

00:06:52.970 --> 00:07:03.250
valvular, atherosclerotic, genetic-- sorry--
structural protein defects or infectious,

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:08.110
which would be lumped into what the medievalist
would call dropsy.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:15.139
So in the precision medicine perspective,
I think we have to become much more careful

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:17.720
about how we label these things.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:26.790
And perhaps less controversial but even more
vivid description of this is dwarfism, which

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:31.620
is often caused by
growth hormone deficiency.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:36.919
And although I have a PhD in computer science,
I am trained in pediatric endocrinology.

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:44.600
And this is a group that endocrinologists
have long been concerned about because the

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:52.039
social and physical challenges of being quite
short can be considerable.

00:07:52.039 --> 00:07:56.270
And therefore, it was a major big breakthrough
when endocrinologists discovered that they

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:05.940
could take the pituitaries from 60,000 cadavers
and extract from these cadavers, from these

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:14.250
pituitaries, the protein, growth hormone,
and then inject them into children who are

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:19.960
going to be much less than 5 foot tall and--
lo and behold-grew to above 5 foot tall.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:30.039
The problem was that because it was extracted
from the 60,000 pituitaries, the fact that

00:08:30.039 --> 00:08:36.979
there were a few individuals with Yacob-Creutzfeldt
diseases, slow virus, resulted in the deaths

00:08:36.979 --> 00:08:40.919
of several children from this disease.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:48.989
And you can guess how those of us-- it's actually
the previous generation before me-- felt about

00:08:48.989 --> 00:08:53.629
having treated these kids with the best of
intent to bring them to normal height-- feeling

00:08:53.629 --> 00:08:58.739
responsible for having caused their death
unintentionally.

00:08:58.739 --> 00:09:09.999
And that created, first of all, a lot of happiness
when we came up with synthetic or bacterially-derived

00:09:09.999 --> 00:09:17.379
recombinant growth hormone that worked extremely
well in making these children grow.

00:09:17.379 --> 00:09:24.879
But because of this-- and I'll get back to
it shortly-- because of this, the place where

00:09:24.879 --> 00:09:29.029
I learned how to practice pediatric endocrinology,
because they had the memory, personal memory

00:09:29.029 --> 00:09:35.920
of this Yacob-Creutzfeldt episode, gave very
low doses of growth hormone because they just

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:39.309
felt that they needed to be conservative.

00:09:39.309 --> 00:09:49.139
Now, in the modern genomic era, a work done
by one of my former Fellows in pediatric endocrinology

00:09:49.139 --> 00:09:57.679
and now team colleague-- Joel Hirschhorn ran
this big study called the GIANT study.

00:09:57.679 --> 00:10:06.470
And what he found was, in the range of normal
height, there are literally hundreds of genes,

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:13.459
maybe 700 genes, that are quite informative
of adult height.

00:10:13.459 --> 00:10:20.670
But each individual gene of those hundreds
of genes bears variants that contribute only

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.860
very, very minutely to the total height.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:30.420
So your overall height from perspective of
these genes is only influenced a very, very

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:33.069
little amount by each gene.

00:10:33.069 --> 00:10:38.660
So this is unlike the diseases that have the
tails at the distribution of height where

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:47.509
extreme short stature is a cause of highly
penetrant small numbers of mutations, sometimes

00:10:47.509 --> 00:10:50.329
a single mutation.

00:10:50.329 --> 00:10:54.129
Which raises the question, what do you call
short stature?

00:10:54.129 --> 00:11:00.239
What do you call that diagnosis when you have
short stature and you're not growth hormone

00:11:00.239 --> 00:11:01.239
deficient?

00:11:01.239 --> 00:11:04.129
What do you call it?

00:11:04.129 --> 00:11:11.259
So it turns out-- for a variety of reasons
which I will make apparent-- we call it idiopathic

00:11:11.259 --> 00:11:12.259
short stature.

00:11:12.259 --> 00:11:17.889
Now, why would you want to have a diagnosis
of idiopathic short stature?

00:11:17.889 --> 00:11:20.110
I think just to label it to be a good doctor.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:29.029
But it turns out that's also useful because
it's a diagnosis that pharmaceutical companies

00:11:29.029 --> 00:11:35.730
can use to drive the prescription of growth
hormone because it may add one or two inches

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:36.730
to height.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.549
prescription of growth hormone because it
may add one or two inches to height.

00:11:38.549 --> 00:11:45.920
And they had a very useful biomarker that's
shown here on the screen which defines height

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.029
with respect to a standard deviation score.

00:11:48.029 --> 00:11:53.949
And that's interesting, of course, because
it means no matter how high the US population

00:11:53.949 --> 00:12:00.489
will be, there will always be people who are
below that threshold and therefore will qualify.

00:12:00.489 --> 00:12:02.670
Which reminds me of a story.

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:16.389
About 25 years ago, when I was a fellow-in-training,
I had submitted an abstract about how with

00:12:16.389 --> 00:12:24.899
our one third of the dose of growth hormone
we were achieving high velocity in adult statures

00:12:24.899 --> 00:12:32.660
that looked just as good as that of the general
population of those with growth hormone deficiency

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.410
who were being treated with growth hormone.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:39.989
And yet we were only using a third of the
dose.

00:12:39.989 --> 00:12:46.429
And to make a long story short, I got a threatening
phone call, as a fellow, from the chief medical

00:12:46.429 --> 00:12:51.129
officer there essentially threatening me to
block my publications because he sat on the

00:12:51.129 --> 00:12:55.230
editorial board of many endocrine journals.

00:12:55.230 --> 00:13:03.429
And this was an unforgettable lesson to me
that tied to a diagnosis and tied to the biomarkers

00:13:03.429 --> 00:13:06.959
around diagnoses are huge amounts of dollars.

00:13:06.959 --> 00:13:14.819
And being able to assign a diagnosis is always--
not always-- is frequently going to be an

00:13:14.819 --> 00:13:20.799
issue not just of science but of finance.

00:13:20.799 --> 00:13:31.249
But also, I want to remind you that not only
insurers or pharmaceutical companies but even

00:13:31.249 --> 00:13:33.279
parents are in a push around the diagnosis.

00:13:33.279 --> 00:13:40.649
So here's a case of a father who comes in
with a 5' 6" child who's still growing.

00:13:40.649 --> 00:13:51.769
But because he wants his son to be an athlete
and, perhaps, a basketball player, he is willing

00:13:51.769 --> 00:13:57.850
to pay out of pocket $45,000 a year it costs
to get a growth hormone.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:00.369
And this raises a bunch of questions.

00:14:00.369 --> 00:14:04.620
A, how does this cause us to stretch our diagnostic
criteria?

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:07.189
And B, is the parent wrong?

00:14:07.189 --> 00:14:14.160
Is this actually a reasonable question and
something that we will have to face a lot

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.899
in the coming years?

00:14:15.899 --> 00:14:23.959
Next, I want to move on to-- in this context
of categorical diagnoses.

00:14:23.959 --> 00:14:27.050
What is the interaction between clinical annotations
and genetics?

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:34.470
And here we're looking at interesting interaction
between genetics and a categorical assessment

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:40.699
of ethnicity or race and a categorical diagnosis.

00:14:40.699 --> 00:14:43.370
And it's hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:48.350
Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, for those of
you who are not clinicians, is a disease that

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:56.379
you've actually heard of about because it
occurs, it's been reported in the newspapers

00:14:56.379 --> 00:15:05.059
when it's famously caused, unfortunately,
athletes to die suddenly on a basketball court.

00:15:05.059 --> 00:15:12.279
And what it is, is an inherited-well, it's
a genetic disease, sometimes inherited-- that

00:15:12.279 --> 00:15:15.860
is also autosomal dominant-- you'll need one
copy of the gene.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:21.589
And it can lead to heart failure, arrhythmias,
obstructed blood flow and sudden cardiac death.

00:15:21.589 --> 00:15:30.230
But the important fact to recall here is,
this is 1 in 500.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:39.310
And my colleague and postdoc Raj Manrai did
a study where he looked at the National Heart,

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:45.139
Lung, and Blood
Institute's ESP study, which used to be thought

00:15:45.139 --> 00:15:52.369
of as big data but at 6,500 individuals, even
with exomes, is no longer considered to be

00:15:52.369 --> 00:15:54.600
large, big data.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:16:03.410
But if we had this disease at 1 in 500, that
would give you about that much red as you

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:08.040
see on that little cartoon of those individuals.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:15.199
Whereas what Raj found was a lot more red
in the population, that is a lot more hypertrophic

00:16:15.199 --> 00:16:22.269
cardiomyopathy than you'd expect for a 1 in
500 disease.

00:16:22.269 --> 00:16:37.279
And when he then looked into what was the
ethnicity or continent of origin of those

00:16:37.279 --> 00:16:49.199
patients, what he found was the following--
an amazing overrepresentation of those individuals

00:16:49.199 --> 00:16:57.459
who are African American And why is that?

00:16:57.459 --> 00:17:07.579
Well, it turns out that individuals, those
who have run the study, had in fact done what

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:12.179
they were supposed to do, which is have ethnically
matched case of the controls.

00:17:12.179 --> 00:17:17.949
So that's probably the case that all the controls
were, in fact, Caucasian and I think they

00:17:17.949 --> 00:17:26.039
sought to have Caucasian or European ancestry
cases.

00:17:26.039 --> 00:17:32.460
But it turns out that, for example, in one
of the studies in Paris, they've included

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:37.880
some individuals who were from Algeria or
from Algerian descent.

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:44.360
And similarly in Boston-- some who had some
relatives who were not from Europe.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:56.179
And what this means is that if you include
in the cases individuals who have recent lineage

00:17:56.179 --> 00:18:04.600
to Africa, then you're going to make it such
that they're going to have a lot of alleles

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:11.580
that are not present in the Caucasian or European
American controls.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:26.419
Because born in Africa were those few families
that thousands of years ago emigrated from

00:18:26.419 --> 00:18:30.640
Africa to Asia and Europe, but just a few
families.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:40.630
And therefore, the representation of African
genetic diversity was extremely sparse.

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:45.509
And therefore, when you do a case control
study and, unbeknownst to you, you have a

00:18:45.509 --> 00:18:54.799
few individuals of African ancestry, of recent
African ancestry, in your cases, you will

00:18:54.799 --> 00:19:03.480
have this overrepresentation of these alleles
which actually have nothing to do with hypertrophic

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:05.890
cardiomyopathy.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:18.380
And lest you think that this is just an interesting
statistical result, shown here from one of

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:24.700
the leading laboratories of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy
are all those individuals who were actually

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:29.050
mistakenly diagnosed as a result of this kind
of mistake.

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:34.549
We've published this, by the way, in the New
England Journal of Medicine last summer.

00:19:34.549 --> 00:19:42.220
And these African American individuals were
not only diagnosed and possibly treated but,

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:46.539
because it's autosomal dominant, family members
were also reached out too.

00:19:46.539 --> 00:19:51.690
Because, of course, if it's autosomal dominant,
a lot family members will carry a putatively

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:54.919
causal variant.

00:19:54.919 --> 00:20:03.010
And unlike this lab which went back and notified
all the families, I'm not sure-- it's hard

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:07.470
to know-- how many of the commercial labs
have done likewise.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:09.570
But note what's happened here.

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:15.679
Because of imprecision of a diagnosis, asserting
that the diagnosis is a unitary thing and

00:20:15.679 --> 00:20:24.420
also asserting that this label of ethnicity
is a unitary thing, we're making inferences

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:29.480
which end up to be damaging.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:35.159
And it continues also with regard to the genetic
part of the reductionism.

00:20:35.159 --> 00:20:41.790
So here we looked up in ClinicalTrials.gov
which studies are being looked at in hypertrophic

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:42.790
cardiomyopathy.

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:48.710
And
there's a trial that is open now.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:57.670
And if you look here in Group 2, there is
a group that has no clinical evidence of ventricular

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:01.169
hypertrophy but has a mutation.

00:21:01.169 --> 00:21:06.710
So they have a complete normal looking hearts,
and you could be as young as 10 years old.

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:13.830
And you'll be receiving this drug for years
as a child, and you've no clinical evidence.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.309
And here's the kicker.

00:21:16.309 --> 00:21:28.379
Of course, if you don't develop disease, you're
going to be considered a therapeutic success.

00:21:28.379 --> 00:21:37.330
Where in fact, as I hope I've just suggested
to you, you may merely be a mislabel.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:41.620
Now, is prediction the acid test of diagnoses?

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:49.260
Often in our papers we like to say, well,
if I'm able to predict this and predict it

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:57.759
reproducibly in multiple populations, perhaps
I'm looking at something causal and that it

00:21:57.759 --> 00:22:00.610
relates in some fundamental way to diagnosis.

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:04.650
I'd like to suggest that that's probably not
the case.

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:09.230
Just stare at this screen for a little bit
and focus on the fact that this is a screen

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:16.840
that was done by Griffin Weber looking at
survival three years after test done.

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:21.340
This is, specifically, white blood cell count,
but Griffin has done this, as you'll see,

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:23.370
on hundreds of other tests.

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:29.940
And what it shows you is your chance of dying,
if you're like me between 50 and 59 years

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:35.190
old and you have a low white blood count between
12 AM and 8 AM, your chance of dying is greater

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:38.330
than 50% in the next three years.

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:44.830
Whereas if it's obtained between 8 AM and
4 PM, the chance of dying is less than 2%.

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:46.289
So why would that be?

00:22:46.289 --> 00:22:48.810
No, it's not a circadian rhythm.

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:54.559
It's because if your blood is drawn at 3 o'clock
in the morning, it means that the doctor is

00:22:54.559 --> 00:22:57.049
extremely worried about you.

00:22:57.049 --> 00:23:03.000
Doctors, contrary to some opinions, are not
sadists and they will not choose to draw blood

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:07.210
at 3 o'clock in the morning unless they're
extremely worried about you.

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:16.120
So rather than treating that variance as noise,
as some data scientists will-- and this is

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:21.810
the part of the problem of not treating clinical
data from a knowledge-based perspective, understanding

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:24.480
how the data was actually obtained.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:31.340
If you didn't know that, you'd treat all the
variants that you see on the screen as noise.

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:34.940
But in fact, it's very, very informative signal.

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:36.740
And it's biased.

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:45.480
So if you treat it as noise, you'll run aground
in some wrong conclusions.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:49.929
Whereas if you understand that there's, on
the one side, patient's pathophysiology, but

00:23:49.929 --> 00:23:56.110
also superimposed what we call health care
system dynamics, which is the behavior of

00:23:56.110 --> 00:24:02.539
the health care system, including informed
doctors trying to do the right thing, then

00:24:02.539 --> 00:24:09.779
only by combining these two signals will you
actually have a good understanding of how

00:24:09.779 --> 00:24:11.210
this relates to the patient.

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:17.030
And in fact, if you see on this graph, on
the x-axis is the odds ratio of death, on

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:28.720
the y-axis is how much of the signal is related
to the lab test itself, you can see that there

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:37.190
are many, many lab tests where merely having
the knowing that test is obtained gives you

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:38.960
a prediction of higher mortality.

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:45.870
Of course, when you combine it-- combine the
lab value with the health system dynamics,

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:54.700
the fact that it was drawn at these times--
then you have a even better signal.

00:24:54.700 --> 00:25:01.080
Next I'm going to go on to how can clinical
data be used to clarify diagnostic boundaries.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:07.590
This is work done by Finale Doshi, who was
a postdoc with me and finished at MIT with

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:14.880
a PhD in computer science and now is at Harvard
School of Engineering and Applied sciences,

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:20.600
has a Assistant Professor of Computer Science.

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:26.159
And when I approached her about the fact that
I was actually bothered by the fact that some

00:25:26.159 --> 00:25:31.799
pediatricians, when they were told by the
mothers that these kids with autism, their

00:25:31.799 --> 00:25:39.740
children, the mothers would say, had these
bowel problems, and they were told by a pediatrician,

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:42.269
"Brain bad, tummy hurts."

00:25:42.269 --> 00:25:46.570
And that's not my words, that's a verbatim
quote.

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:51.389
So what Finale did was a very nice data-driven
approach-- using the clinical data obtained

00:25:51.389 --> 00:25:55.720
from many of chronic health records, using
the distributed query system called SHRINE

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:59.190
that goes over many i2b2 systems.

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:01.169
But I don't want to distract you with how
we got the data.

00:26:01.169 --> 00:26:05.080
Suffice it to say, we had data on thousands
of individuals with autism.

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:13.220
And what Finale did is created a bit-vector
for each of these patients chunked into one

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:17.429
vector for each six month block, all the way
to 15 years.

00:26:17.429 --> 00:26:26.159
And each bit in the bit-vector describes whether
that child would have one of 5,000 different

00:26:26.159 --> 00:26:30.100
diagnoses in that six month block.

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:40.019
So you can think of each block as having 5,000
bits, on and on for 15 years.

00:26:40.019 --> 00:26:50.519
And when she did that and applied an unsupervised
clustering algorithm to these patients longitudinal

00:26:50.519 --> 00:26:56.059
data obtained from electronic health record,
what she found was quite interesting.

00:26:56.059 --> 00:27:02.679
What she found was one group with a lot of
epilepsy-- greater than 80% of epilepsy, as

00:27:02.679 --> 00:27:08.129
opposed to the background rate of 20% epilepsy
in general.

00:27:08.129 --> 00:27:15.659
She found another subgroup with a lot of infections
like otitis media and sinusitis.

00:27:15.659 --> 00:27:20.999
And-- although it's not shown here, it was
statistically significant, but not prevalent

00:27:20.999 --> 00:27:25.690
enough to make it onto the graph-- a number
of autoimmune diseases such as inflammatory

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:30.830
bowel disease, Crohn's, all sorts of colitis
and type 1 diabetes.

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:43.850
And then there was a third subgroup with ADHD,
anxiety and-- not shown here for some of the

00:27:43.850 --> 00:27:50.090
reasons, it was not frequent enough but it
was significant-- schizophrenia and major

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:51.090
depression.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:57.700
So what you have here are three very distinct
biologies, three very distinct clinical courses

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:04.259
of three populations that if you just labeled
them as autism and did the case control with

00:28:04.259 --> 00:28:12.139
them, you would be mixing different biologies,
as in the heart failure example I gave you

00:28:12.139 --> 00:28:14.879
earlier on.

00:28:14.879 --> 00:28:22.029
So that's saying that clinical data may, in
fact, help us separate these underlined diagnoses.

00:28:22.029 --> 00:28:29.290
And note that we used an unsupervised method
to define from a datadriven perspective what

00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:33.029
might be the distinct subgroups there.

00:28:33.029 --> 00:28:38.139
And can we do similar things using biology
and genes?

00:28:38.139 --> 00:28:44.169
So, for example, with Dr. Nazeen, who at a
time was a

00:28:44.169 --> 00:28:49.269
graduate student in Bonnie Burger's group
at MIT, we did a study looking at gene expression

00:28:49.269 --> 00:28:56.610
in one of the pathways that we had uncovered
in that earlier autism expression study that

00:28:56.610 --> 00:29:01.390
I told you about-- namely, the innate immune
pathway.

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:08.340
And so we took the KEGG innate immune pathway
and we sent across a whole bunch of diseases

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.820
that are shown on the right.

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:13.730
We posted this in Genome Biology.

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:20.249
To what extent are these genes dysregulated
in these different diseases?

00:29:20.249 --> 00:29:23.451
As you can see here, there's quite a lot of
overlap.

00:29:23.451 --> 00:29:31.710
But a way to, perhaps, quantify it better
is to just ask, how does gene expression just

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:35.409
in that pathway classify all those different
diseases?

00:29:35.409 --> 00:29:43.320
And although it's not at the level of clinical
action ability, what you do see here is that

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:54.279
all of those diseases can be classified, somewhat
effectively, with errors in the curve, specifically

00:29:54.279 --> 00:30:00.019
different from random, and some of them quite
a bit so, just on base of that innate immune

00:30:00.019 --> 00:30:01.019
pathway.

00:30:01.019 --> 00:30:06.220
So that tells us there's a lot of biological
overlap between these different diseases.

00:30:06.220 --> 00:30:13.419
And that's important not only for classification
but thinking about not only the mechanism

00:30:13.419 --> 00:30:16.820
of disease but how you're going to treat it
and, perhaps, repurpose drugs that are used

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:20.340
to treat one disease to another disease.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:26.940
And really ask the question-- for example,
in the psychiatric diseases, psychiatrists

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:37.559
are very fast to acknowledge that there is
a fairly significant overlap in the symptomatology

00:30:37.559 --> 00:30:40.049
of these different diseases.

00:30:40.049 --> 00:30:50.009
And because, as I've, I hope, convinced you,
there is a lot of financial incentives to

00:30:50.009 --> 00:30:56.259
have different diagnoses as well as personal
incentives to have different diagnoses.

00:30:56.259 --> 00:31:03.799
You can better appreciate how some of these
psychiatric diseases such as autism-- or neurodevelopmental

00:31:03.799 --> 00:31:12.860
diseases such as autism-- when there was a
reclassification of autism in the DSM-5 manual,

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:18.020
there was an enormous furor nationwide.

00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:25.759
And on the one hand, I want to smile because
I often tease Mark Musen about how arcane

00:31:25.759 --> 00:31:28.360
some of his work on ontologies are.

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:36.549
But in this case, the ontology of the DSM-5
was making the front page of many newspapers

00:31:36.549 --> 00:31:43.410
because it was so important how patients were
being piled into that different diagnoses.

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:49.580
But note that what happens when you pile individuals
into this categorical diagnosis, it causes

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:55.210
different financial and social effects to
be met.

00:31:55.210 --> 00:32:04.110
And the claim that I hope I'm beginning to
bolster is that from a data-driven perspective,

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:12.499
we can do a far more transparent and robust
job of defining what actually constitutes

00:32:12.499 --> 00:32:15.159
these diseases.

00:32:15.159 --> 00:32:20.649
So as I argued just now, there's implications
for study and for treatment.

00:32:20.649 --> 00:32:26.220
You might want to study not all autisms compared
to, let's say, neurotypical individuals but

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:30.460
pick a subgroup that are much more biologically
similar to one another, just as you would

00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:32.970
for heart failure.

00:32:32.970 --> 00:32:42.049
And if you went to a genetics-first approach,
it would not be apparent that there are these

00:32:42.049 --> 00:32:46.890
subsets, so the scene was just not strong
enough from a genetics-first or genetics-only

00:32:46.890 --> 00:32:47.890
approach.

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:54.110
And clinicians might miss it because even
if you had a huge practice of autism, you

00:32:54.110 --> 00:33:00.519
might easily miss the fact that there was
this overlap in this patient of, let's say,

00:33:00.519 --> 00:33:02.080
inflammatory bowel disease.

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:07.321
Because even if you have a lifetime practice
of 1,000 children with autism, which, by the

00:33:07.321 --> 00:33:10.750
way, is a phenomenally large practice, you
probably wouldn't expect to have more than

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:12.789
10 individuals of inflammatory bowel disease.

00:33:12.789 --> 00:33:19.740
So over a lifetime, you might just ascribe
that to random bad luck.

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:24.179
And then I just want to emphasize again the
unsupervised nature of the example I gave

00:33:24.179 --> 00:33:32.649
you about clustering the electronic health
records versus the self-referential and circular

00:33:32.649 --> 00:33:34.779
supervised labeling.

00:33:34.779 --> 00:33:42.889
Because if we start with a definitional categorical
diagnosis, we're going to be stuck with all

00:33:42.889 --> 00:33:49.679
the consequences of that, which, by the way--
if we use artificial intelligence techniques,

00:33:49.679 --> 00:33:54.110
as we're beginning to do, such as deep learning
against images, but we're using as the gold

00:33:54.110 --> 00:34:04.799
standard our categorical diagnoses-- we are
in some sense going to be limiting the efficacy

00:34:04.799 --> 00:34:09.500
with this big data of our diagnostic support.

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:18.480
Because these big data algorithms will be
limited to the supposed gold standard definitions

00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:20.280
through the supervised learning.

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:28.599
And so again, what I'm trying to argue for
is let's look at longitudinal large datasets

00:34:28.599 --> 00:34:36.490
on populations to drive in a much more objective
way what constitutes our diagnoses.

00:34:36.490 --> 00:34:44.710
And I also want to argue for a multimodal-first
exploration that is not necessarily just going

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:48.470
phenotype-first or genetics-first.

00:34:48.470 --> 00:34:55.359
But as many people on this phone call have,
in fact, in their own work illustrated, going

00:34:55.359 --> 00:35:02.700
at both-- in that case, both modalities, but
if there are more than two, in multiple modalities

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:07.360
at the same time-- and in our modern machine
learning frameworks there are no theoretical

00:35:07.360 --> 00:35:10.410
reasons, at least, why we should not do that.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:13.800
What about when there is no diagnosis?

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:20.780
How can we use clinical data and big data,
perhaps, to get there?

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:25.549
So this is where I'm very proud, actually,
to describe my involvement in something called

00:35:25.549 --> 00:35:32.290
the Undiagnosed Diseases Network, which is
an offshoot from the very successful Undiagnosed

00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:41.349
Diseases Program at the NIH, where we said--
and I'm a PI on the Coordinating Center--

00:35:41.349 --> 00:35:48.500
where we conceived of this network as being
hub-hosted where all that data, all the genomic

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:57.260
data, would be stored in the cloud or all
the clinical data would be stored in a cloud.

00:35:57.260 --> 00:36:04.670
And those of you who are involved with human
study subjects trials will recognize that

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:07.990
it was a major achievement for us to get,
all of a sudden, academic health centers,

00:36:07.990 --> 00:36:15.799
including the NIH, to agree to a single protocol
that A, said that they would go with this

00:36:15.799 --> 00:36:21.349
shared data resource, that everybody within
the network would be able to see the data.

00:36:21.349 --> 00:36:27.819
And furthermore, a patient could be referred
into this network and then go wherever they

00:36:27.819 --> 00:36:32.950
needed to be referred to within this network,
so going from one hospital to a completely

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:37.280
different hospital system-- something that
does not happen usually.

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:42.930
By doing this, by taking these patients who
have had already, prior to their application

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:49.170
to this network, a Hejira through multiple
health care systems trying to get a diagnosis,

00:36:49.170 --> 00:36:59.869
we were able to therefore obtain genome, whole
genome sequencing or exome sequencing, phenotypic

00:36:59.869 --> 00:37:07.940
evaluation and coding per the HPO terminology,
and referring these patients to the right

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:11.680
academic health centers where the right individual
can evaluate them.

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:16.319
I should point out in this network we also
have two sequencing centers.

00:37:16.319 --> 00:37:23.670
We also have a biological validation because
you can appreciate that many mutations that

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:28.940
look like there may be loss of function often
don't create loss of function.

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:34.850
So Drosophila and zebrafish core we're using
CRISPR.

00:37:34.850 --> 00:37:41.290
They knock in the human mutation equivalent
into these organisms to see if it causes a

00:37:41.290 --> 00:37:46.859
phenotype, so we can better assess the pathogenicity
of these variants.

00:37:46.859 --> 00:37:54.890
And the point is, based on the approach that
was pioneered at the UDP we're finding that

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:58.119
we're making a lot of diagnoses.

00:37:58.119 --> 00:37:59.480
And this is now out of date.

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:07.130
We have received well over, I think, 1,100
applications, and over 500 or 600 are under

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:15.950
review, and, actually, over 500 participants
have been accepted, and, I think, on the order

00:38:15.950 --> 00:38:20.530
of 80 new diagnoses have been made.

00:38:20.530 --> 00:38:26.970
When there's no diagnosis, what we're doing
now is, if there's a high probability causal

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:32.460
variant, we're going to link- as we have for,
I think, on the order of 80 patients of this

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:37.710
new diagnostic label-- linking the clinical
findings in that patient to that variant.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:40.910
But I think we have to be vigilant.

00:38:40.910 --> 00:38:45.510
Because do all individuals with that variant
have disease?

00:38:45.510 --> 00:38:53.740
I think the probands that we've identified
do have a causal link from that variant to

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:56.170
that disease.

00:38:56.170 --> 00:39:01.470
But how does genetic background, with individuals'
different genetic background and different

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:09.900
environmental exposures, have a phenotype
that's affected by that variant?

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:17.370
And another way I can say it is, we know intuitively
and mathematically that the probability of

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:21.089
disease given the variant is not the same
thing as the probability of the variant given

00:39:21.089 --> 00:39:22.089
the disease.

00:39:22.089 --> 00:39:24.890
But in practice, we don't behave as if we
know this.

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:31.950
And for a long time, there was a number of
variants in the HFE gene which were considered

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:35.740
to be pathogenic for hemochromatosis.

00:39:35.740 --> 00:39:42.270
And indeed, if you look at hereditary hemochromatosis
clinics, 80% of individuals in those clinics

00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:50.760
will have one of a small number of variants
in each HFE mutation, in each HFE gene.

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:56.710
But then a study was done, an amazing study
was done at Kaiser Permanente by researchers

00:39:56.710 --> 00:40:07.770
from Scripps where they genotyped-- I believe
it was-- over 44,000 individuals with hemochromatosis.

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:16.049
And they found, I think, 151 who were homozygous
for one of those mutations.

00:40:16.049 --> 00:40:21.180
And of those 151 only one had any clinical
evidence or found a history of hemochromatosis.

00:40:21.180 --> 00:40:31.000
So the penetrance went from 80% in the hereditary
hemochromatosis clinic to a less than 1%.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:40.010
And that's because the individuals at Kaiser
had very different genetic backgrounds and

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:41.810
very different exposures.

00:40:41.810 --> 00:40:48.140
And in case that doesn't convince you, some
other papers which looked at three different

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:55.799
mouse strains with HFE mutation-- of those
mouse strains only one of the three mouse

00:40:55.799 --> 00:40:59.289
strains showed evidence of hemochromatosis.

00:40:59.289 --> 00:41:02.309
So
the point is, we are making these new diagnoses

00:41:02.309 --> 00:41:08.670
in the Undiagnosed Diseases Network, but we
have to be careful not to make the same error.

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:15.450
And the way to avoid making that same error
is going to be the emergence of all these

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:22.190
large-scale population screening-- sorry--
genomic sequencing, which will give us what

00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:27.020
are the probabilities of those variants in
the general population so that we can have

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:29.940
a better sense of how they are linked to health
and disease.

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:34.310
In closing, I want to ask the following question.

00:41:34.310 --> 00:41:39.210
Why is medicine so dependent on categorical
diagnoses?

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:44.750
Well, there was a question that was asked
in the New England Journal of Medicine 33

00:41:44.750 --> 00:41:45.750
years ago.

00:41:45.750 --> 00:41:51.990
They basically asked-- let me-- they literally
asked, if you have a disease that's 1 in 1,000

00:41:51.990 --> 00:41:57.530
and you have a test that has a sensitivity
of a 100% and have a specificity of 95%--

00:41:57.530 --> 00:42:03.950
so that's a very good looking test, 100% sensitive,
95% specific.

00:42:03.950 --> 00:42:08.920
Very few genetic tests can even approach that
level of performance.

00:42:08.920 --> 00:42:17.390
So with this 1 in 1,000 disease, what's the
probability of having disease if you test

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:18.829
positive in that test?

00:42:18.829 --> 00:42:24.260
A bunch of Harvard doctors and students were
asked to study it 33 years ago and reported

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:25.980
upon it in the New England Journal.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:29.190
And About half of the doctors answered, greater
than 90%.

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:33.970
And half of the doctors answered, around 1%
to 2%.

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:37.410
And just for those of you who are falling
asleep, I'll just remind you that right answer

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:43.890
is-- if you've 1,000 individuals, one will
be a true positive because it's 1 in 1,000

00:42:43.890 --> 00:42:44.890
disease.

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:48.710
And because it's 95% specific, you'll have
50 false positives.

00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:53.000
So you'll have 51 positives total, only one
of them are true.

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:58.480
So if you tested positive on that genetic
test with those remarkable characteristics,

00:42:58.480 --> 00:43:03.859
nonetheless, your chance of having the disease
is less than 2%.

00:43:03.859 --> 00:43:10.640
33 years later we ran the same test on heart
doctors and students, and we essentially found

00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:12.400
exactly the same distribution.

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:18.780
But because we were using the same question,
we've been publishing not in the New England

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:20.250
Journal but in Journal of Internal Medicine.

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:26.240
So my point is this-- human beings are not
good probabilistic machines.

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:33.410
We We're not innate Bayesian machines intuitively.

00:43:33.410 --> 00:43:39.079
And therefore, having a categorical diagnosis
is really cognitively necessary for us.

00:43:39.079 --> 00:43:44.900
But that creates a real problem when we're
entering this age of millions of genetic tests.

00:43:44.900 --> 00:43:50.660
How are we going to navigate the fact that
we're not natural Bayesian machines?

00:43:50.660 --> 00:43:56.380
I will now summarize so that I can get to
any questions.

00:43:56.380 --> 00:44:02.020
Our addiction to categorical diagnoses is
cognitively useful and will likely continue

00:44:02.020 --> 00:44:03.549
for that reason.

00:44:03.549 --> 00:44:08.539
But categorical diagnosis can result in spurious
biological and clinical inference.

00:44:08.539 --> 00:44:15.380
And it's often manipulated for dollars or
for a non-pecuniary secondary agenda.

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:22.210
I want to point out and remind us that the
prediction of diagnostic class does not necessarily

00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:25.319
provide evidence of biological etiology.

00:44:25.319 --> 00:44:30.230
And I hope I've begun to convince you, at
least, that diagnoses are more robust when

00:44:30.230 --> 00:44:35.160
they're data-driven, when you formally model
health system dynamics for clinically acquired

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:38.829
data, that it's statistically-informed, that
in the

00:44:38.829 --> 00:44:42.740
spirit of the precision medicine report, when
it's multimodal, when you're looking at more

00:44:42.740 --> 00:44:48.510
than one data type at a time, you're actually
going to do better.

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:58.460
And to avoid circularity of this diagnostic
categorical problem having unsupervised or

00:44:58.460 --> 00:45:02.430
lightly supervised approach rather than a
fully supervised approach may be advisable.

00:45:02.430 --> 00:45:09.220
And the cause, the first point-- because of
the cognitive utility of categorical diagnoses

00:45:09.220 --> 00:45:15.340
I think we have very little choice at this
point, other than to recognize the necessity

00:45:15.340 --> 00:45:23.109
of having computational decision support that
will, actually, substitute for our non-Bayesian

00:45:23.109 --> 00:45:31.270
wetware with computational software and hardware
that does in fact do the appropriate probabilistic

00:45:31.270 --> 00:45:37.670
assessment of post-test probability of various
disease combinations.

00:45:37.670 --> 00:45:42.510
And with that, I want to thank you for your
patience and thank my collaborators for their

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:43.510
work.

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:46.000
And I'll stop here and I'll see if there are
any questions.

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:55.220
And I'll leave it to Mark to be master of
ceremonies.

00:45:55.220 --> 00:45:56.220
Thank you, Zak.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:57.220
That was a great talk.

00:45:57.220 --> 00:46:00.869
I really appreciate that, as do all the people
listening on the webinar.

00:46:00.869 --> 00:46:05.309
As you know, there is a box where you can
type in your questions.

00:46:05.309 --> 00:46:09.720
And what I will do is try to synthesize and
restate the questions so we can have a little

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:16.790
bit of order as we ask Zak to expand on some
of the issues that he's raised today.

00:46:16.790 --> 00:46:20.270
So please think about your questions, put
them into the question box.

00:46:20.270 --> 00:46:22.950
And while you're doing that, let me ask Zak.

00:46:22.950 --> 00:46:28.580
You've raised lots of issues about the messiness
with which we categorize disease, with the

00:46:28.580 --> 00:46:35.320
messiness with which we record data, basically
with all the complexities of clinical information.

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:39.309
And I'm just wondering, what are the implications
of all that for electronic health records?

00:46:39.309 --> 00:46:45.049
If we could start from scratch, how would
you design an EHR to accommodate all of this?

00:46:45.049 --> 00:46:51.420
Well, first of all, I want to paint a positive
version of that.

00:46:51.420 --> 00:46:56.270
The same argument can be made about claims
data.

00:46:56.270 --> 00:46:58.780
Claims data are comprehensive so they don't
have esteemed bias.

00:46:58.780 --> 00:47:02.079
But they're certainly biased for financial
gain.

00:47:02.079 --> 00:47:06.150
And there's only more core screened than many
EHRs.

00:47:06.150 --> 00:47:14.280
But smart and meticulous individuals have
been able to use the claims data to answer

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:21.390
real questions by combining in various creative
probabilistic fashion different claims codes

00:47:21.390 --> 00:47:24.099
to identify the right patient's populations.

00:47:24.099 --> 00:47:30.690
Similarly, I will say that you can use, perhaps,
using some of the methods I referred to but

00:47:30.690 --> 00:47:35.549
not necessarily-- you can actually go against
the existing messy data, codified data, in

00:47:35.549 --> 00:47:43.160
a variety of ways to obtain some probabilistic
estimates of what the underlying true patient

00:47:43.160 --> 00:47:44.680
[INAUDIBLE] are.

00:47:44.680 --> 00:47:51.359
But to answer your question directly, if I
were, perhaps, at Google, which I'm not, I

00:47:51.359 --> 00:47:56.349
might argue that their ontologists will wipe
the floor with you regularly just by brute

00:47:56.349 --> 00:48:03.450
force machine learning, statistical machine
learning, over the textual corpora.

00:48:03.450 --> 00:48:11.770
And we actually never fully-- not until the
very end do we any labeling of ontologies,

00:48:11.770 --> 00:48:19.470
but we actually calculate patients' similarity
across, for example, all the word vectors,

00:48:19.470 --> 00:48:21.440
across all these patients.

00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:28.020
So I would say that I would not redesigned
the EHRs from scratch because we've already

00:48:28.020 --> 00:48:30.830
spent several billion
dollars.

00:48:30.830 --> 00:48:37.010
But I would, perhaps, in addition to being
optimistic on how we can use codified data,

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:41.970
I think there is a much more modern statistical
framework.

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:47.319
If you look at the clinical nodes, that will
be quite helpful.

00:48:47.319 --> 00:48:54.690
Ironically, actually, some of the cut-and-paste
facilities in EHRs will actually take away

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:59.770
from some of the signal in these clinical
nodes.

00:48:59.770 --> 00:49:05.020
Because, of course, people like cutting and
pasting but it, of course, takes away a lot

00:49:05.020 --> 00:49:11.569
of signal from the richness that normally
is in human text.

00:49:11.569 --> 00:49:12.719
Thanks.

00:49:12.719 --> 00:49:14.210
What There are two questions.

00:49:14.210 --> 00:49:18.049
You get these requests to put up your conclusions
slide against so people can take a look at

00:49:18.049 --> 00:49:19.460
those bullet points.

00:49:19.460 --> 00:49:20.460
Already.

00:49:20.460 --> 00:49:24.690
Can you just back up your side?

00:49:24.690 --> 00:49:27.089
And question from an ontology lover.

00:49:27.089 --> 00:49:32.690
"What is your advice to librarians working
with EMR bank, REDCap groups, et cetera, given

00:49:32.690 --> 00:49:34.920
our tendency to categorize?

00:49:34.920 --> 00:49:38.920
How can we help our patrons think and plan
for a new paradigm?

00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:43.300
We work with researchers, physicians who contribute
to the classification, reclassification efforts

00:49:43.300 --> 00:49:45.280
of diseases.

00:49:45.280 --> 00:49:46.280
What would you tell these people?"

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:47.950
I'll tell them two things.

00:49:47.950 --> 00:49:56.119
First of all, a very tactical thing to do
is to say, why don't you have your researchers

00:49:56.119 --> 00:50:05.799
at least analyze in two ways-- the way they
originally did it, but then use a set of categorizations

00:50:05.799 --> 00:50:13.029
that lump together similar things relatively
robustly like PheWAS codes.

00:50:13.029 --> 00:50:18.970
PheWAS codes were developed at Vanderbilt
in order for them to be able to look at different

00:50:18.970 --> 00:50:25.840
diseases because, of course, ICD-9 does not
do a great job of maintaining that kind of

00:50:25.840 --> 00:50:27.319
hierarchy.

00:50:27.319 --> 00:50:30.060
But even PheWAS itself is insufficient.

00:50:30.060 --> 00:50:36.660
But I would say that if we use that or whatever
ontology, Mark, you would-you're far more

00:50:36.660 --> 00:50:40.359
experienced then I am-- would point to, but
I found that PheWAS codes, by the way, are,

00:50:40.359 --> 00:50:43.130
actually, quite useful in rolling it up.

00:50:43.130 --> 00:50:54.480
You can see whether it's finding robust association,
robust cluster rather than just an artifact

00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:56.470
of the ontology.

00:50:56.470 --> 00:51:03.540
The harder way to do it-- but the one that's,
I think, much more consonant with our BD2K

00:51:03.540 --> 00:51:08.349
spirit-- is to actually use the primary data
itself and say, first, let's classify the

00:51:08.349 --> 00:51:15.630
data by the primary data processing, principle
component analysis, or some other dimensionality

00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:23.490
reduction technique to see if these patients
that we've labeled in a certain way are, in

00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:25.240
fact, clustered together.

00:51:25.240 --> 00:51:29.369
Because that will give you a good smell test
whether the categorization that you're using

00:51:29.369 --> 00:51:33.940
is, in fact, robust relative to the question
you're asking.

00:51:33.940 --> 00:51:38.740
There's an interesting corollary question
that comes from Jim Semino.

00:51:38.740 --> 00:51:43.829
What are the implications for race as a demographic?

00:51:43.829 --> 00:51:52.210
I think that this is a very, very tough one
because I think I showed you how not doing

00:51:52.210 --> 00:51:55.680
this in one way created a lot
of problems.

00:51:55.680 --> 00:51:58.260
And so we're stuck with the following.

00:51:58.260 --> 00:52:06.210
The good news used to be that self-identified
race correlates well with what the EHR thinks

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:07.550
your race is.

00:52:07.550 --> 00:52:13.319
In other words, administrators are tracking
patients somewhat.

00:52:13.319 --> 00:52:20.349
The problem is that ethical work, as well
as we've come in increasingly multiracial

00:52:20.349 --> 00:52:23.089
society.

00:52:23.089 --> 00:52:33.700
So I think that what we're going to have to
start doing is using different modalities

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:37.680
depending on what the purpose is.

00:52:37.680 --> 00:52:42.720
So if you really want to find continent of
origin, probably looking at a few hundred

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:45.890
SNPs-- and that's all it takes these days.

00:52:45.890 --> 00:52:53.849
Just a few hundred SNPs have been identified
that give fairly reliable continent of origin

00:52:53.849 --> 00:52:57.090
and will give you the mixture of continental
origin.

00:52:57.090 --> 00:53:00.579
Those of you who've used 23andMe can see that.

00:53:00.579 --> 00:53:06.680
And I think that's the answer, by the way,
of how we will have to categorize patients.

00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:13.619
So if you want to do a case control study,
as has been done in population genetics now

00:53:13.619 --> 00:53:25.940
for a long time but not in clinical medicine,
we'll have to compare populations on those

00:53:25.940 --> 00:53:28.549
genetic backgrounds as determined by the SNPs.

00:53:28.549 --> 00:53:33.520
But that only satisfies a certain class of
questions.

00:53:33.520 --> 00:53:39.440
There are other classes of questions that
are still going to be much more informed by

00:53:39.440 --> 00:53:40.500
what your identification is.

00:53:40.500 --> 00:53:43.190
And that's a much more complicated story.

00:53:43.190 --> 00:53:50.440
And I'm sure we could have, and perhaps should
have, a much longer discussion about.

00:53:50.440 --> 00:53:54.619
Another questioner wants to know what are
the implications for gender and sex, given

00:53:54.619 --> 00:53:59.359
that we're moving into an era where gender
and sex should not be viewed as binary classifications.

00:53:59.359 --> 00:54:06.730
Well, speaking as a pediatric endocrinologist,
I think that's a very good question.

00:54:06.730 --> 00:54:15.980
And certainly, just having the male and female
genders on the electronic health record for

00:54:15.980 --> 00:54:21.140
the most part is not very helpful in supporting
that diagnose.

00:54:21.140 --> 00:54:36.260
Now, I know that the EHRs have changed recently
to support that multiplicity of gender assignments.

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:42.710
But it's not clear how they're being informed--
whether by a patient, by the clinician.

00:54:42.710 --> 00:54:49.030
So if we want to use these for studies, I
think somebody's going to have to come together

00:54:49.030 --> 00:54:59.150
and decide how we're going to represent it
in a way that reflects both, and maybe simultaneously,

00:54:59.150 --> 00:55:03.910
biological measurements and patient identity.

00:55:03.910 --> 00:55:14.559
Because we recognize that in this identity
there is what your sexual organs look like,

00:55:14.559 --> 00:55:20.319
what your chromosomal arrangement looks like,
where your mutations are affecting or not

00:55:20.319 --> 00:55:26.930
different hormones that are associated with
different traditional gender identities.

00:55:26.930 --> 00:55:35.310
So again, I think that there's a truly biological
part of this that I think we can and should

00:55:35.310 --> 00:55:38.010
capture in EHRs.

00:55:38.010 --> 00:55:43.470
But then there's also the self-identification
part, which I know is complicated but, I think,

00:55:43.470 --> 00:55:49.099
also does inform us about some aspects of
the patient that we have to also figure out

00:55:49.099 --> 00:55:50.890
how to capture in the EHR.

00:55:50.890 --> 00:55:53.290
And I'm not clear that we have done that yet.

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:54.980
I agree.

00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:57.279
Zak, we've reached the end of the hour.

00:55:57.279 --> 00:56:00.150
And I want to thank you for really illuminating
talk.

00:56:00.150 --> 00:56:04.470
If you look at the questions that are still
posted, you'll see a number of listeners have

00:56:04.470 --> 00:56:09.029
basically just posted a thank you because
this is such a stimulating conversation.

00:56:09.029 --> 00:56:13.410
Really want to thank you for your contribution
and thank you everyone who dialed in today.

00:56:13.410 --> 00:56:15.450
Thank you very much for your attention.

